 note = {Revisions were a recurring theme in Clementi’s career. They emerge as the “central ‘problem’ posed by his methods as a composer, publisher and editor” (Stewart-MacDonald, 2002). Similarly to his celebrated op. 2, Clementi “updated” and “improved” his Progressive Sonatinas op. 36 multiple times over a span of more than twenty years from 1797 to c. 1820. Both revision processes seem to reflect evolving ideas about performance styles and notation, as well as a strong affinity with the ever-developing English piano manufacturing industry during the composer’s lifetime. Rather than marketing strategies that facilitate the pursuit of financial gain (Platinga 1977) or “disturb [...] the original effect of the music” (Wollenberg 2018), I view revisions as opportunities for updating musical notation to the most recent available pianos, “born from a unique negotiation between original and ‘new and improved’” (Helyard 2022).
When it comes to fingered publications, Clementi’s predilection for revision becomes all the more relevant, as his fundamental system of fingering—the one that had defined his own success as a pianist—remained intact. Following the revised edition of his excruciatingly difficult Toccata op. 11 (1792), where he revealed the secrets of this success for the first time, op. 36 was his second publication with fingerings, functioning as a supplement to his method op. 42 and kickstarting a whole series of didactic works—opp. 42, 43, and 44. With his revisions not only did Clementi satisfy the English amateur’s craving for novelty, but he also provided aspiring keyboardists of his time with the tools to conquer difficulty in music systematically.
Using examples from Clementi’s piano method (1801), I will focus on Clementi’s fingering system, which revolves around interconnecting hand positions that must remain in close contact with the keys. I will demonstrate the crucial role of such a system in the act of reading a musical text, and will examine changes in fingering and articulation between the first and fifth editions of op. 36 (1797/c. 1815) in relation to the elusive “original effect of the music” and to changing instrumental affordance.},
 publisher = {CCHK Cornell University},
 title = {With considerable improvements: English Pianos with Additional Keys and Fingering in Clementi’s Revised Progressive Sonatinas, Op. 36},
 type = {non-written output},
